  {
    "pkattachmentId": 33593,
    "bookid": 32,
    "chapterId": 15,
    "fileId": 7626,
    "type": "WSI",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<p>High grade ductal carcinoma in situ with necrosis in several expanded ducts within fibrotic breast stroma. Malignant in situ carcinoma cells show marked nuclear pleomorphism with mitoses. A large duct is similarly affected by high grade DCIS, displaying luminal haemorrhage and surrounded by inflamed fibrous stroma containing foamy histiocytes and cholesterol clefts.</p>",
    "source": "<p>SGH Pathology</p>",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/7626.dzi",
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 33594,
    "bookid": 32,
    "chapterId": 15,
    "fileId": 7627,
    "type": "WSI",
    "diagnosis": "<p>Low and intermediate nuclear grade DCIS, dimorphic pattern.</p>",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<p>Many ducts are expanded by a dimorphic population of monomorphic cells which are centrally darker with smaller hyperchromatic nuclei, and peripherally paler with larger vesicular nuclei. Immunohistochemistry shows diffuse nuclear reactivity for ER, while CK5/6 and CK14 decorate the intact rim of myoepithelial cells without any staining of the epithelial population, corroborating the diagnosis of DCIS of low to intermediate nuclear grade, without necrosis. Papillary DCIS with a similar neoplastic population is present. Occasional calcifications are discerned.</p>",
    "source": "<p>SGH Pathology</p>",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/7627.dzi",
    "thumbnail": null
  }
